gm-method (a static-host deploy 'keeps going out of date despite pushing'): the cause is almost always that the DEPLOY WORKFLOW is FAILING on recent pushes - the site freezes at the last SUCCESSFUL build while every new push's deploy errors. Diagnose with the run history (gh run list --workflow=<deploy>): a run of greens then a wall of failures pinpoints the breaking commit. Common break: a consumer imports a sibling-SDK subpath (e.g. 'pkg/new-module') that exists only in the LOCAL un-published sibling, but the build npm-installs the PUBLISHED package which lacks it -> the bundler (esbuild) 'Could not resolve' -> deploy fails. FIXES, most-robust-first: (1) deterministically STAGE the missing files + their package.json `exports` entries into the build's dist/node_modules in the build step (vendor copies in the repo) - works regardless of npm publish timing or postinstall/cp quirks; (2) a postinstall vendor-bridge that copies them into node_modules (can be missed if the build copies node_modules->dist via a cp loop with version quirks); (3) publish the sibling (slow; needs the dep bump after). VERIFY 'green != live-updated': after the run goes green, curl the LIVE bundle and grep for a signature of the new code (the deploy may inline modules, so the standalone file 404s but its code is IN the bundle). LESSON: any unresolved import = a SILENT site-freeze; keep the deploy green or the site never updates.